# UNO CLI Refactoring Report

## Overview

This report describes the refactoring process performed on the UNO CLI project.
The goal of the refactor was to improve code readability, maintainability, extensibility, and overall project structure without changing the core gameplay behavior.

---

# Objectives

The refactoring process focused on the following objectives:

* Reduce code duplication
* Improve class and method organization
* Separate gameplay logic into clearer responsibilities
* Improve naming consistency
* Make future feature additions easier
* Preserve existing functionality through characterization testing

---

# Problems Identified in Original Implementation

## 1. Large Monolithic Game Class

The original implementation contained a very large `Game` class responsible for:

* player management
* deck management
* turn handling
* input processing
* card validation
* scoring
* AI behavior
* special card logic

This violated the Single Responsibility Principle and made the code difficult to maintain.

---

## 2. Repeated Conditional Logic

Many gameplay checks used repeated `if-else` structures for:

* card color checking
* card type handling
* special effects
* wildcard processing

This increased complexity and reduced readability.

---

## 3. Weak Separation of Concerns

Game state, UI interaction, and rule logic were tightly coupled together.
This made testing and extension difficult.

---

## 4. Magic Values and Hardcoded Strings

Card names, colors, and actions were often represented directly as raw strings throughout the codebase.

Examples:

* `"RED"`
* `"DRAW_TWO"`
* `"SKIP"`

This increased risk of bugs caused by typos and inconsistent usage.

---

# Refactoring Changes

## 1. Extracted Helper Methods

Large methods were divided into smaller reusable methods.

Examples include:

* turn progression helpers
* card validation methods
* deck reshuffling logic
* winner checking
* special effect handling

Benefits:

* improved readability
* easier debugging
* easier unit testing

---
## 2. Centralized Card Logic

Card effect handling was consolidated into dedicated logic sections instead of being spread across multiple gameplay methods.

This reduced duplicated code and simplified future rule extensions.

---

## 3. Better Turn Management

Turn handling logic was reorganized to clearly support:

* normal direction
* reverse cards
* skipped turns
* draw penalties
* AI turns
* multiplayer progression

Benefits:

* easier debugging
* cleaner gameplay flow

---

## 4. Reduced Code Duplication

Repeated blocks for:

* drawing cards
* validating playable cards
* applying special actions

were extracted into reusable methods.

Benefits:

* smaller codebase
* lower maintenance cost

---

## 5. Improved Data Organization

Collections such as:

* player hands
* deck
* discard pile
* player metadata

were reorganized for better clarity and easier access.

---

# Characterization Testing

Before refactoring, characterization testing was used to preserve existing behavior.

The following gameplay behaviors were verified:

* turn progression
* card drawing
* reverse card handling
* skip card behavior
* draw two behavior
* wild card behavior
* winner detection
* deck reshuffling
* AI move handling

This ensured that refactoring did not unintentionally change gameplay functionality.

---

# Benefits Achieved

## Maintainability

The project is now significantly easier to modify and debug.

## Readability

Smaller methods and improved naming make the code easier to understand.

## Extensibility

The new structure allows easier addition of:

* new game modes
* additional card types
* improved AI
* GUI integration
* networking features

## Reliability

Cleaner logic organization reduces the likelihood of introducing bugs during future development.

---

# Remaining Improvement Opportunities

Potential future improvements include:

* introducing dedicated `Card`, `Deck`, and `Player` classes
* replacing string-based cards with enums or objects
* adding automated unit tests
* separating CLI UI from game engine logic
* implementing save/load functionality
* adding multiplayer networking
* improving AI strategy

---

# Conclusion

The refactoring successfully improved the internal structure of the UNO CLI project while preserving gameplay behavior.

The updated codebase is now:

* easier to understand
* easier to extend
* easier to debug
* more maintainable for future development

The project is better prepared for future feature additions and long-term maintenance.
